From: hmec...@googlegroups.com [hmec...@googlegroups.com] on behalf of Marc-Olivier Beausoleil [beauso...@gmail.com]

Sent: 23 June 2017 21:11

To: hmecology: Hierarchical Modeling in Ecology

Cc: beauso...@gmail.com
Subject: Re: Data augmentation

So, I understand now that the seasons will be the same across all individuals for a particular capture occasion. The temperature was probably a bad example but I have "individual" data, such as the location (GPS) of the sample, the state the sample (fresh and not fresh), quantitative data about the place it was collected (degree of the slope), etc. I'm questioning the data augmentation in this particular case. How do you augment a specific covariate, whatever that covariate might be.

I feel that augmenting for example a individual quantitative data is not trivial. NAs are not working in JAGS. 0 means that it's explicitly nothing. So how do you augment the data with a continuous variable that was recorded for each individual?

On Friday, June 23, 2017 at 11:43:01 AM UTC-4, marc.kery wrote:
> Now you lose me. Isn't it the case that you have a total of 203 occasions on which you tried to detect these beasts ? You detected a total of 31, but you wonder how many more you may have missed on the 203 occasions altogether ? And you also would want to estimate the effects of factors that affect detection probability, such as season and temperature.
>
> So isn't it the case these covariates are constant within one day ? I.e., any one of these 203 days belongs to a particular season and has some particular temperature reading (or for the latter you have to decide on one value, perhaps the mean temp) ? If all of this is true, then you simply take Excel for instance, and copy the values of a covariate within a column as far down as you need to have the same number of rows in your covariate matrix as in your augmented detection history matrix.
